## sn45.154
sn45.154:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose a spike of rice or barley was pointing the right way. If you trod on it with hand or foot, it’s possible it may break the skin and produce blood. | T: “It’s possible, monks, that a well-aimed spike of bearded wheat or bearded barley, when pressed by a hand or foot, will cut into the hand or foot and draw blood.
sn45.154:1.2 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn45.154:1.3 | S: Because the spike is pointing the right way. | T: Because of the well-aimed-ness of the spike.
sn45.154:1.4 | S: In the same way, a mendicant whose view and development of the path is pointing the right way may well break ignorance, produce knowledge, and realize extinguishment. | T: In the same way, it is possible that a monk, through well-aimed view, through a well-aimed development of the path, will cut into ignorance, give rise to clear knowing, and realize unbinding.
sn45.154:1.5 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn45.154:1.6 | S: Because their view is pointing the right way. | T: Because of the well-aimed-ness of the view.
sn45.154:1.7 | S: And how does a mendicant whose view and development of the path is pointing the right way break ignorance, give rise to knowledge, and realize extinguishment? | T: “And how does a monk, through well-aimed view, through a well-aimed development of the path, cut into ignorance, give rise to clear knowing, and realize unbinding?
sn45.154:1.8 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: There is the case where a monk develops right view dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go. He develops right resolve… right speech… right action… right livelihood… right effort… right mindfulness… right concentration dependent on seclusion… dispassion… cessation, resulting in letting go.
sn45.154:1.9 | S: That’s how a mendicant whose view and development of the path is pointing the right way breaks ignorance, gives rise to knowledge, and realizes extinguishment.” | T: This is how a monk, through well-aimed view, through a well-aimed development of the path, cuts into ignorance, gives rise to clear knowing, and realizes unbinding.”
sn45.154:1.10 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

