sn45.2:3.4 | S: right speech … | T: right speech…
sn45.2:3.5 | S: right action … | T: right action…
sn45.2:3.6 | S: right livelihood … | T: right livelihood…
sn45.2:3.7 | S: right effort … | T: right effort…
sn45.2:3.8 | S: right mindfulness … | T: right mindfulness…
sn45.2:3.9 | S: right immersion, which relies on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripens as letting go. | T: right concentration dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in relinquishment.
sn45.2:3.10 | S: That’s how a mendicant with good friends develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path. | T: This is how a monk who has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, develops & pursues the noble eightfold path.
sn45.2:4.1 | S: And here’s another way to understand how good friends are the whole of the spiritual life. | T: “And through this line of reasoning one may know how having admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues is actually the whole of the holy life:
sn45.2:4.2 | S: For, by relying on me as a good friend, sentient beings who are liable to rebirth, old age, and death, to sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress are freed from all these things. | T: It is in dependence on me as an admirable friend that beings subject to birth have gained release from birth, that beings subject to aging have gained release from aging, that beings subject to death have gained release from death, that beings subject to sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair have gained release from sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, & despair.
sn45.2:4.3 | S: This is another way to understand how good friends are the whole of the spiritual life.” | T: It is through this line of reasoning that one may know how having admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues is actually the whole of the holy life.”
sn45.2:4.4 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.4
sn45.4:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Sāvatthī.
sn45.4:1.2 | S: Then Venerable Ānanda robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then Ven. Ānanda early in the morning adjusted his under robe and—carrying his bowl & outer robes—went into Sāvatthī for alms.
sn45.4:1.3 | S: He saw the brahmin Jānussoṇi driving out of Sāvatthī in an all-white chariot drawn by mares. | T: He saw the brahman Jāṇussoṇin leaving Sāvatthī in an all-white chariot drawn by mares.
sn45.4:1.4 | S: The yoked horses were pure white, as were the ornaments, chariot, upholstery, reins, goad, and canopy. And his turban, robes, sandals were white, as was the chowry fanning him. | T: White were the yoked horses, white the ornaments, white the chariot, white the upholstery, white the reins, white the goad, white the canopy, white his turban, white his clothes, white his sandals, and with a white yak-tail fan he was fanned.
sn45.4:1.5 | S: When people saw it they exclaimed, | T: Seeing him, people were saying,
sn45.4:1.6 | S: “Wow! That’s a divine vehicle! | T: “What a sublime vehicle!
sn45.4:1.7 | S: It’s a vehicle fit for divinity!” | T: What a sublime-looking vehicle!”
sn45.4:2.1 | S: Then Ānanda wandered for alms in Sāvatthī. After the meal, on his return from almsround, he went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened, adding, | T: Then Ven. Ānanda, having gone for alms in Sāvatthī, after the meal, returning from his alms round, went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
sn45.4:3.1 | P: “Idhāhaṁ, bhante, pubbaṇhasamayaṁ nivāsetvā pattacīvaramādāya sāvatthiṁ piṇḍāya pāvisiṁ. | T: “Just now, lord, early in the morning, I adjusted my under robe and—carrying my bowl & outer robes—went into Sāvatthī for alms.
sn45.4:3.2 | P: Addasaṁ khvāhaṁ, bhante, jāṇussoṇiṁ brāhmaṇaṁ sabbasetena vaḷavābhirathena sāvatthiyā niyyāyantaṁ. | T: I saw the brahman Jāṇussoṇin leaving Sāvatthī in an all-white chariot drawn by mares.
sn45.4:3.3 | P: Setā sudaṁ assā yuttā honti setālaṅkārā, seto ratho, setaparivāro, setā rasmiyo, setā patodalaṭṭhi, setaṁ chattaṁ, setaṁ uṇhīsaṁ, setāni vatthāni, setā upāhanā, setāya sudaṁ vālabījaniyā bījīyati. | T: White were the yoked horses, white the ornaments, white the chariot, white the upholstery, white the reins, white the goad, white the canopy, white his turban, white his clothes, white his sandals, and with a white yak-tail fan he was fanned.
sn45.4:3.4 | P: Tamenaṁ jano disvā evamāha: | T: Seeing him, people were saying,
sn45.4:3.5 | P: ‘brahmaṁ vata, bho, yānaṁ. | T: ‘What a sublime vehicle!
sn45.4:3.6 | P: Brahmayānarūpaṁ vata, bho’ti. | T: What a sublime-looking vehicle!’
sn45.4:3.7 | S: “Sir, can you point out a divine vehicle in this teaching and training?” | T: Is it possible to designate a sublime vehicle in this Dhamma-Vinaya?”
sn45.4:4.1 | S: “I can, Ānanda,” said the Buddha. | T: “It is possible, Ānanda,” said the Blessed One.
sn45.4:4.2 | S: “These are all terms for the noble eightfold path: | T: “That is a synonym for this very same noble eightfold path:
sn45.4:4.3 | S: ‘divine vehicle’, or else ‘vehicle of truth’, or else ‘supreme victory in battle’. | T: ‘sublime vehicle,’ ‘Dhamma-vehicle,’ ‘unexcelled victory in battle.’”
sn45.4:5.1 | S: When right view is developed and cultivated it culminates with the removal of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: “Right view, Ānanda, when developed & pursued, has the subduing of passion as its end-point, the subduing of aversion as its end-point, the subduing of delusion as its end-point.
sn45.4:5.2 | S: When right purpose … | T: “Right resolve…
sn45.4:5.3 | S: right speech … | T: Right speech…
sn45.4:5.4 | S: right action … | T: Right action…
sn45.4:5.5 | S: right livelihood … | T: Right livelihood…
sn45.4:5.6 | S: right effort … | T: Right effort…
sn45.4:5.7 | S: right mindfulness … | T: Right mindfulness…
sn45.4:5.8 | S: right immersion is developed and cultivated it culminates with the removal of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: Right concentration, when developed & pursued, has the subduing of passion as its end-point, the subduing of aversion as its end-point, the subduing of delusion as its end-point.
sn45.4:6.1 | S: This is a way to understand how these are all terms for the noble eightfold path: | T: “It is by this sequence of reasons that one can know how that is a synonym for this very same noble eightfold path:
sn45.4:6.2 | S: ‘divine vehicle’, or else ‘vehicle of truth’, or else ‘supreme victory in battle’.” | T: ‘sublime vehicle,’ ‘Dhamma-vehicle,’ ‘unexcelled victory in battle.’”
sn45.4:6.3 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn45.4:7.1 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said that, the One Well-Gone, the Teacher, said further:
sn45.4:8.1 | S: “Of which the qualities of faith and wisdom | T: One with the qualities of conviction & discernment
sn45.4:8.2 | S: are always yoked to the shaft; | T: always yoked to its shaft,
sn45.4:8.3 | S: conscience its draft-pole, mind its strap, | T: shame its pole, the heart its yoke-tie,
sn45.4:8.4 | S: mindfulness its careful driver; | T: mindfulness the protective charioteer,
sn45.4:9.1 | S: the chariot equipped with ethics, | T: virtue the chariot-accessories,
sn45.4:9.2 | S: its axle absorption, energy its wheel; | T: jhāna the axle, persistence the wheels,
sn45.4:9.3 | S: equanimity and immersion its draft-pole, | T: equanimity the balance of the yoke,
sn45.4:9.4 | S: desirelessness its upholstery; | T: hungerless-ness its upholstery,
sn45.4:10.1 | S: of which good will, harmlessness, | T: non-ill will, harmlessness,
sn45.4:10.2 | S: and seclusion are its weaponry; | T: & seclusion its weapons,
sn45.4:10.3 | S: endurance, its shield and armor: | T: patience its armor & shield:
sn45.4:10.4 | S: to sanctuary from the yoke it rolls— | T: It rolls to security from bondage.
sn45.4:11.1 | S: this, originating in oneself, | T: Coming into play from within oneself:
sn45.4:11.2 | S: is the supreme divine vehicle. | T: the sublime vehicle unsurpassed.
sn45.4:11.3 | S: The attentive leave the world in it, | T: They, the enlightened, leave the world.
sn45.4:11.4 | S: sure of winning the victory.” | T: They, absolutely, win victory.
sn45.4:11.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.56
sn45.56:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, the dawn is the forerunner and harbinger of the sunrise. | T: “Monks, this is the forerunner, the harbinger of the rising of the sun, i.e., dawnrise.
sn45.56:1.2 | S: In the same way good friendship is the forerunner and harbinger of the noble eightfold path for a mendicant. | T: In the same way, this is the forerunner, the harbinger of the arising of the noble eightfold path in a monk, i.e., admirable friendship.
sn45.56:1.3 | S: A mendicant with good friends can expect to develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path. | T: It can be expected of a monk who has an admirable friend that he will develop the noble eightfold path, that he will pursue the noble eightfold path
sn45.56:1.4 | S: And how does a mendicant with good friends develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path? | T: “And how does a monk with admirable friendship develop the noble eightfold path, pursue the noble eightfold path?
sn45.56:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which culminate in the removal of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: “There is the case where a monk develops right view ending in the subduing of passion, ending in the subduing of aversion, ending in the subduing of delusion. He develops right resolve… right speech… right action… right livelihood… right effort… right mindfulness… right concentration ending in the subduing of passion, ending in the subduing of aversion, ending in the subduing of delusion.
sn45.56:1.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant with good friends develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path.” | T: “This, monks, is how a monk with admirable friendship develops the noble eightfold path, pursues the noble eightfold path.”
sn45.56:1.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


