## sn45.62
sn45.62:1.1 | S: “… accomplishment in rational application of mind. | T: “Monks, this is the forerunner, the harbinger of the rising of the sun, i.e., dawnrise. In the same way, this is the forerunner, the harbinger of the arising of the noble eightfold path in a monk, i.e., virtue-consummation… desire-consummation… self-consummation [according to the Commentary, this means being consummate in the training of the mind]… view-consummation… heedfulness-consummation… appropriate attention.
sn45.62:1.2 | S: A mendicant accomplished in rational application of mind can expect to develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path. | T: It can be expected of a monk who has appropriate attention that he will develop the noble eightfold path, that he will pursue the noble eightfold path.
sn45.62:1.3 | S: And how does a mendicant accomplished in rational application of mind develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path? | T: “And how does a monk with appropriate attention develop the noble eightfold path, pursue the noble eightfold path?
sn45.62:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which culminate in the removal of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: “There is the case where a monk develops right view ending in the subduing of passion, ending in the subduing of aversion, ending in the subduing of delusion. He develops right resolve… right speech… right action… right livelihood… right effort… right mindfulness… right concentration ending in the subduing of passion, ending in the subduing of aversion, ending in the subduing of delusion.
sn45.62:1.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant accomplished in rational application of mind develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path.” | T: “This, monks, is how a monk with appropriate attention develops the noble eightfold path, pursues the noble eightfold path.”
sn45.62:1.6 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)
sn45.62:1.7 | P: Sūriyapeyyālaṁ. | T: (none)
sn45.62:2.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn45.62:2.1 | P: Kalyāṇamittaṁ sīlañca, | T: (none)
sn45.62:2.2 | P: chando ca attasampadā; | T: (none)
sn45.62:2.3 | P: Diṭṭhi ca appamādo ca, | T: (none)
sn45.62:2.4 | P: yoniso bhavati sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.8
sn45.8:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: I have heard that at one time the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. There he addressed the monks, “Monks!” “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to him. The Blessed One said,
sn45.8:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach and analyze for you the noble eightfold path. | T: “I will teach & analyze for you the noble eightfold path.
sn45.8:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn45.8:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to him.
sn45.8:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
sn45.8:2.1 | S: “And what is the noble eightfold path? | T: “Now what, monks, is the noble eightfold path?
sn45.8:2.2 | S: It is right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: Right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn45.8:3.1 | S: And what is right view? | T: “And what, monks, is right view?
sn45.8:3.2 | S: Knowing about suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: Knowledge with regard to [or: in terms of] stress, knowledge with regard to the origination of stress, knowledge with regard to the stopping of stress, knowledge with regard to the way of practice leading to the stopping of stress:
sn45.8:3.3 | S: This is called right view. | T: This, monks, is called right view.
sn45.8:4.1 | S: And what is right purpose? | T: “And what, monks, is right resolve?
sn45.8:4.2 | S: The purpose of renunciation, good will, and harmlessness. | T: Resolve for renunciation, resolve for non-ill will, resolve for harmlessness:
sn45.8:4.3 | S: This is called right purpose. | T: This, monks, is called right resolve.
sn45.8:5.1 | S: And what is right speech? | T: “And what, monks, is right speech?
sn45.8:5.2 | S: Refraining from speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical. | T: Abstaining from lying, abstaining from divisive speech, abstaining from harsh speech, abstaining from idle chatter:
sn45.8:5.3 | S: This is called right speech. | T: This, monks, is called right speech.
sn45.8:6.1 | S: And what is right action? | T: “And what, monks, is right action?
sn45.8:6.2 | S: Refraining from killing living creatures, stealing, and unchastity. | T: Abstaining from taking life, abstaining from stealing, abstaining from sexual intercourse:
sn45.8:6.3 | S: This is called right action. | T: This, monks, is called right action.
sn45.8:7.1 | S: And what is right livelihood? | T: “And what, monks, is right livelihood?
sn45.8:7.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple gives up wrong livelihood and earns a living by right livelihood. | T: There is the case where a disciple of the noble ones, having abandoned dishonest livelihood, keeps his life going with right livelihood.
sn45.8:7.3 | S: This is called right livelihood. | T: This, monks, is called right livelihood.
sn45.8:8.1 | S: And what is right effort? | T: “And what, monks, is right effort?
sn45.8:8.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant generates enthusiasm, tries, makes an effort, exerts the mind, and strives so that bad, unskillful qualities don’t arise. | T: (i) There is the case where a monk generates desire, endeavors, activates persistence, upholds & exerts his intent for the sake of the non-arising of evil, unskillful qualities that have not yet arisen.
sn45.8:8.3 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen are given up. | T: (ii) He generates desire, endeavors, activates persistence, upholds & exerts his intent for the sake of the abandoning of evil, unskillful qualities that have arisen.
sn45.8:8.4 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities arise. | T: (iii) He generates desire, endeavors, activates persistence, upholds & exerts his intent for the sake of the arising of skillful qualities that have not yet arisen.
sn45.8:8.5 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities that have arisen remain, are not lost, but increase, mature, and are fully developed. | T: (iv) He generates desire, endeavors, activates persistence, upholds & exerts his intent for the maintenance, non-confusion, increase, plenitude, development, & culmination of skillful qualities that have arisen.
sn45.8:8.6 | S: This is called right effort. | T: This, monks, is called right effort.
sn45.8:9.1 | S: And what is right mindfulness? | T: “And what, monks, is right mindfulness?
sn45.8:9.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (i) There is the case where a monk remains focused on the body in & of itself—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn45.8:9.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (ii) He remains focused on feelings in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn45.8:9.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (iii) He remains focused on the mind in & of itself—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn45.8:9.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (iv) He remains focused on mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn45.8:9.6 | S: This is called right mindfulness. | T: This, monks, is called right mindfulness.
sn45.8:10.1 | S: And what is right immersion? | T: “And what, monks, is right concentration?
sn45.8:10.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: (i) There is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
sn45.8:10.3 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, they enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: (ii) With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance.
sn45.8:10.4 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, they enter and remain in the third absorption, where they meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: (iii) With the fading of rapture, he remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, and senses pleasure with the body. He enters & remains in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’
sn45.8:10.5 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, they enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: (iv) With the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—he enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
sn45.8:10.6 | S: This is called right immersion.” | T: This, monks, is called right concentration.” That is what the Blessed One said. Gratified, the monks delighted in the Blessed One’s words.
sn45.8:10.7 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn46.11
sn46.11:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, whatever living creatures engage in the four postures— | T: “Monks, just as the living beings that assume the four postures—
sn46.11:1.2 | S: sometimes walking, sometimes standing, sometimes sitting, sometimes lying down—all do so depending on the earth and grounded on the earth. | T: at times walking, at times standing, at times sitting down, at times lying down—all assume the four postures in dependence on the earth, established on the earth;
sn46.11:1.3 | S: In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics. | T: in the same way, it’s in dependence on virtue, established on virtue, that a monk develops the seven factors for awakening & pursues the seven factors for awakening.
sn46.11:2.1 | S: And how does a mendicant develop the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics? | T: “And how is it that a monk—in dependence on virtue, established on virtue—develops the seven factors for awakening & pursues the seven factors for awakening?
sn46.11:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, | T: “There is the case where a monk develops mindfulness as a factor for awakening dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go. He develops analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening… persistence as a factor for awakening… rapture as a factor for awakening… calm as a factor for awakening… concentration as a factor for awakening…
sn46.11:2.3 | S: and equanimity, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: equanimity as a factor for awakening dependent on seclusion, dependent on dispassion, dependent on cessation, resulting in letting go.
sn46.11:2.4 | S: That’s how a mendicant develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics.” | T: “It’s in this way that a monk—in dependence on virtue, established on virtue—develops the seven factors for awakening & pursues the seven factors for awakening.”
sn46.11:2.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn46.14
sn46.14:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn46.14:1.2 | S: Now at that time Venerable Mahākassapa was staying in the Pipphali cave, and he was sick, suffering, gravely ill. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Mahā Kassapa was staying in the Pepper Tree Cave: diseased, in pain, severely ill.
sn46.14:1.3 | S: Then in the late afternoon, the Buddha came out of retreat, went to Venerable Mahākassapa, sat down on the seat spread out, and said to him: | T: Then the Blessed One, emerging from his seclusion in the evening, went to Ven. Mahā Kassapa and, on arrival, sat down on a seat made ready. Having sat down, he said to Ven. Mahā Kassapa,
sn46.14:2.1 | S: “I hope you’re keeping well, Kassapa, I hope you’re all right. I hope your pain is fading, not growing; that its fading is evident, not its growing.” | T: “I hope you are getting better, Kassapa. I hope you are comfortable. I hope that your pains are lessening and not increasing. I hope that there are signs of their lessening, and not of their increasing.”
sn46.14:2.2 | S: “Sir, I’m not keeping well, I’m not getting by. My pain is terrible and growing, not fading; its growing is evident, not its fading.” | T: “I am not getting better, lord. I am not comfortable. My extreme pains are increasing, not lessening. There are signs of their increasing, and not of their lessening.”
sn46.14:3.1 | S: “Kassapa, I’ve rightly explained these seven awakening factors. When developed and cultivated, they lead to direct knowledge, to awakening, and to extinguishment. | T: “Kassapa, these seven factors for awakening rightly taught by me, when developed and pursued, lead to direct knowledge, to self-awakening, to unbinding.
sn46.14:3.2 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
sn46.14:3.3 | S: The awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, and equanimity. | T: “Mindfulness as a factor for awakening rightly taught by me, when developed and pursued, leads to direct knowledge, to self-awakening, to unbinding. “Analysis of qualities as a factor for awakening, rightly taught by me, when developed and pursued, leads to direct knowledge, to self-awakening, to unbinding. “Persistence as a factor for awakening.… “Rapture as a factor for awakening.… “Calm as a factor for awakening.… “Concentration as a factor for awakening.… “Equanimity as a factor for awakening rightly taught by me, when developed and pursued, leads to direct knowledge, to self-awakening, to unbinding.
sn46.14:3.4 | S: These are the seven awakening factors that I’ve rightly explained. When developed and cultivated, they lead to direct knowledge, to awakening, and to extinguishment.” | T: “Kassapa, these are the seven factors for awakening rightly taught by me that—when developed and pursued—lead to direct knowledge, to self-awakening, to unbinding.”
sn46.14:3.5 | S: “Indeed, Blessed One, these are awakening factors! | T: “They are indeed factors for awakening, O Blessed One.
sn46.14:3.6 | S: Indeed, Holy One, these are awakening factors!” | T: They are indeed factors for awakening, O One Well-Gone.”
sn46.14:4.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: That is what the Blessed One said.
sn46.14:4.2 | S: Satisfied, Venerable Mahākassapa approved what the Buddha said. | T: Gratified, Ven. Mahā Kassapa delighted in the Blessed One’s words.
sn46.14:4.3 | S: And that’s how he recovered from that illness. | T: And Ven. Mahā Kassapa recovered from his disease.
sn46.14:4.4 | P: Tathāpahīno cāyasmato mahākassapassa so ābādho ahosīti. | T: That was how Ven. Mahā Kassapa’s disease was abandoned.
sn46.14:4.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)


